1          /************************************************************************************************
2           USING DIGIT SELECTORS IN THE PICTURE STATEMENT
3               This program uses options to customize formats that display numeric data.
4               Keywords: PROC FORMAT, FORMAT, PICTURE
5               SAS Versions: SAS 9, SAS Viya
6               Documentation:
6        ! https://documentation.sas.com/?cdcId=pgmsascdc&cdcVersion=default&docsetId=proc&docsetTarget=p1xidhqypi0fnwn1if8opjpqpbmn
6        ! .htm
7               1. The picture statement creates an template for a numeric format that can be used to display data. The template
8                  is defined using 0s and 9s where the 9 represents a digit that must be displayed while 0 represents a digit
9                  that is displayed if it exists.
10              2. Use the LOW and HIGH keywords to indicate that any non-missing value should be displayed.
11              3. For Nonzero-digit selectors, values are printed with leading zeros. For Zero digit selectors only those positions
12                 that have values are printed. For example if the number is 123 and the format specifies 0009, the value will be
13                 printed as 123 but if there is a nonzero selector - 9999 - it will be printed as 0123.
14              4. The noedit option specifies that numbers are not digit selectors, but messaging characters. Trailing characters
14       ! are
15                 not part of the template and are displayed as entered.
16              5. In the PROC REPORT compare the results for using the format vs not using it.
17         ************************************************************************************************/
18         data delay;
19            infile datalines;
20            input delay destination $3.;
21            datalines;

